BT /F1 10 Tf 12 TL 56 760 Td (revenue from recipe kits and ingredient suppliers, though we do not rely on it in our) Tj T* (projections.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Competition) Tj T* () Tj T* (There are two kinds of alternatives. The first is the manual hydrometer, which is cheap but) Tj T* (tedious, risks contamination, and gives only occasional snapshots. The second is a small number) Tj T* (of existing electronic fermentation monitors. These tend to cost two to four times as much as) Tj T* (Brewbeacon, often require the device to float inside the beer, and mostly display numbers) Tj T* (without telling the brewer what to do. Several are aimed at serious or semi-professional) Tj T* (brewers rather than the broad hobbyist base.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our advantage is the combination of three things: a non-contact clip-on design, a low price) Tj T* (point, and coaching rather than raw data. Coaching is also the hardest part for a competitor to) Tj T* (copy, because it improves as we gather more batch data. Every batch our users brew makes the) Tj T* (advice better for everyone, which builds a data advantage over time.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Team) Tj T* () Tj T* (Maya Okonkwo-Reyes, chief executive, is a senior studying business and a lifelong homebrewer) Tj T* (who has brewed over sixty batches. She leads strategy, customer research, and partnerships.) Tj T* (Daniel Thackeray, chief technology officer, is a computer engineering student who designed the) Tj T* (sensor hardware and firmware. Priya Venkataraman, head of data, is a statistics graduate) Tj T* (student who built the fermentation models behind the coaching engine. Luis Ferreira-Nakamura,) Tj T* (head of product and design, is a design student who created the app and the clip mechanism. We) Tj T* (also have informal guidance from a faculty advisor in food science at our university and two) Tj T* (experienced brewing club members who act as test brewers.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Milestones and Financials) Tj T* () Tj T* (Over the past year we defined the concept, built a working prototype, and placed it with our) Tj T* (first external users. Forty-two home brewers have used Brewbeacon for ten weeks across 118) Tj T* (batches. In that group, 31 have paid, bringing in 2,139 dollars in hardware sales and 1,290) Tj T* (dollars in annualized subscription revenue. Users reported that Brewbeacon helped them catch a) Tj T* (problem in 37 percent of batches, and 88 percent said they would be disappointed if they could) Tj T* (no longer use it.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Our prototypes are currently assembled by hand at a cost of about 58 dollars per unit. A first) Tj T* (production run of 1,000 units would reduce this to about 22 dollars. We project 400 sensors) Tj T* (sold in the first twelve months after launch, growing to 3,500 in year two, with subscription) Tj T* (revenue rising from about 6,000 dollars in year one to over 60,000 dollars in year two. We) Tj T* (expect to reach break-even on operating costs late in year two. We have not yet formed a legal) Tj T* (entity and plan to incorporate once we secure funding.) Tj T* () Tj T* (What We Are Asking For) Tj T* () Tj T* (We are seeking 40,000 dollars. About 24,000 dollars would fund the first production run of) Tj T* (1,000 units, including tooling and certification testing. About 8,000 dollars would go to app) Tj T* (development and cloud infrastructure, and the remainder to customer acquisition through brewing) Tj T* (clubs and hobbyist communities and to legal costs of forming the company. Beyond funding, we) Tj T* (would value mentorship in hardware manufacturing and introductions to brewing retailers and) Tj T* (supply distributors. With this support, we believe Brewbeacon can become the standard tool that) Tj T* (helps home brewers make the beer they intended, every time.) Tj ET
endstream
